# Why Dependency Direction Matters

Dependency direction is one of the most important—and most frequently misunderstood—
architectural decisions in software systems.

When dependency direction is left unconstrained, systems tend to degrade into tightly
coupled structures where change becomes risky and costly. This subsection explains why
explicit dependency direction is essential for maintaining architectural integrity over
time.

---

## Unrestricted Dependencies Lead to Entanglement

In systems without defined dependency direction, any component can reference any other.
While this may appear flexible initially, it leads to increasing entanglement as the
system grows.

Over time, components become mutually dependent, making it difficult to understand
responsibilities or isolate the impact of change.

Dependency direction exists to prevent this form of architectural erosion.

---

## Change Propagation Is a Structural Problem

When dependencies flow indiscriminately, change propagates unpredictably.

A modification in one part of the system may require changes in many unrelated areas,
not because of business requirements, but because of structural coupling.

By constraining dependency direction, change propagation becomes predictable and
contained within defined boundaries.

---

## Stability Depends on Dependency Control

Stable systems depend on stable dependencies.

If high-level policies depend on volatile implementation details, stability is lost.
Conversely, when volatile details depend on stable abstractions, the system can evolve
without constant disruption.

Dependency direction ensures that stability flows inward rather than outward.

---

## Dependency Direction Enables Independent Work

Clear dependency direction allows teams to work independently within different parts
of the system.

When dependencies are constrained, teams can modify implementations without coordinating
changes across the entire codebase. This is critical for scaling development efforts.

---

## Dependency Direction Is a Design Commitment

Dependency direction does not emerge accidentally. It must be deliberately designed and
consistently enforced.

This architecture treats dependency direction as a core design commitment rather than a
byproduct of code organization. Subsequent subsections explain how dependency direction
is enforced and how it shapes the layered model.

